Testing The Democratic Temper Of ‘We, The People Of India’
Move To End Authoritarian Control Of CEC
The Press Note https://t.co/Bno0t0YcA6 dated 26th September 2026 issued by the Election Commission in response to the Indian Express expose of 22nd Sept 2026 is the clearest admission of guilt dressed up as a proactive Election Commission (Commission). Apparently the two Election Commissioners (ECs) Sukhbir Singh Sandhu and Vivek Joshi have put their foot down in a faceoff with the CEC following the Express expose, and forced the CEC and the Commission to release this Note to:  (a) admit, indirectly, that the mandate of the Chief Election Commissioner and Other Election Commissioners Appointment, Conditions of Service and Term of Office Act, 2023 was being flouted with regard to issues mentioned in the Press Note; and (b) to clearly state that henceforth all decisions of the Commission shall be taken according to the 2023 Act as mentioned in the Note, with their consent of the 2 ECs.

Below is itemized the admissions referred above and the resolve to tread  the path of inclusivity, decentralization of SIR exercise, cooperative and joint control (of CEC & 2 ECs), and rectitude (procedural and substantive) in following the mandate of the 2023 Act. At least that is what it appears as of now.

One: Resolve To Decentralise The SIR Exercise
According to the Press Note: “In case of any person to whom notice has been issued during the ongoing SIR, for being unmapped and logical discrepancies, BLOs will visit the homes of such persons for collection of documents and thereafter upload them on the ECINet, for decision by the ERO. Such persons need not be called for hearing to ERO/AERO office. Only in exceptional circumstances, as decided by the ERO, the hearing will be held preferably online. Any adult member of the family can be authorized by the elector to attend the hearing on their behalf, if required. Facilities for the online hearing to be strengthened. DEOs (District Election Officers) will create adequate number of help desks/hold special camps for the people living in night shelters, labor, poor people, homeless etc. as per requirement.”
This is all very good, but why now? Because of the expose! The damage has already been done in Bengal and Bihar.

Two: Unlawful Work Distribution Orders 
The Note says: “The work re- distribution orders issued by the concerned officer were not actually implemented after the orders of the two Commissioners and the oversight over the IT division by the DEC was never actually withdrawn.”
That means that the work distribution order were issued without the approval of the two Election Commissioners.

Three: IT Head Was Allowed To Run Amock
Continuing, the Note says: “and the oversight over the IT division by the DEC was never actually withdrawn”. The oversight may not have been withdrawn but it was not actually implemented and the IT head was apparently running amock with her centralization, denying access to the BLOs and EROs to the EC portal ECINet as alleged in the Express expose.

Four: Reference To Supreme Court Decision on Form 6 Is A Red Herring
Regarding Form 6, the Note says: “The declaration attached to Form 6 for SIR has been upheld by the Hon’ble Supreme Court. For non-SIR period, the Forms applicable as per Rules (the Registration of Electors Rules 1960) will be used.” The Election Commission is referring to the Supreme Court’s judgment of 27 May 2026.

A two-judge Bench of Chief Justice Surya Kant and Justice Joymalya Bagchi decided a batch of petitions challenging the Special Intensive Revision (SIR) of electoral rolls that began in Bihar (including Association for Democratic Reforms v. Election Commission of India, W.P. (C) 640/2025). The Court upheld the SIR as a valid exercise of power under Article 324 and Section 21(3) of the Representation of the People Act, 1950. On Form 6, the Court held that the statutory Form 6 under the Registration of Electors Rules, 1960 is designed for ordinary revision under Section 21(2) and need not be applied literally to an intensive revision. Indeed, it said that the Commission has residual authority to frame a documentation requirement suited to SIR.

The Express Report of 22nd September 2026 says “In July this year, as first reported by The Indian Express, a form that every adult citizen has to fill to become a voter started asking a question it never did earlier. Linking the application to the SIR, it asked if the new voter or their parents/ grand- parents “exist” in the “electoral roll of last SIR.” Applicants could not proceed without answering it. This despite the fact that in May, Joshi had warned on file that Form 6 could not be changed in this manner without the government amending the rules, and Sandhu had agreed. The change was made anyway. After The Indian Express first reported this change in July, Sandhu put on record on August 13 that it was “unauthorized and illegal” and “must be removed immediately”. Until Monday morning, that question was still there.”

The charge against the Commission  is that the Form 6 was amended to include this question  without the express approval of the 2 ECs. In that way it was unlawful. The Supreme Court upholding Form 6  does not cure the illegality of changing the Form 6 without the approval of the 2 ECs. Reference to Supreme Court Judgement is a red herring. The May 2026 judgment upheld the SIR framework and the Bihar Annexure D declaration. But it did not separately examine later changes made to the online Form 6 on ECINET (reportedly in July 2026), which ask for parents’/grandparents’ details from the last SIR. Those changes came after the judgment. The Court also said this residual power is “not unbounded” and must remain within the existing statutory scheme.

Five: Involvement of independent experts from IIT should have been done earlier
The Note says “Field officers have role-based access to the ECINET system as per their statutory powers. A Committee headed by Sr. Deputy Election Commissioner (DEC) and including an independent expert from IIT/IIIT will review the ECINET to double-check that it complies with the provisions of the Acts and Rules and submit report to the Commission.”
But this should have been done before the SIR exercise was completed in several states especially West Bengal and Bihar. The damage of removing voters from the voter list through centralization, and denying them their right to vote cannot be undone.  

Six: New initiatives on IT modules & portals were taken without the approval of the Commission 
The Note says: “Henceforth, all the new initiatives about IT modules and portals will be discussed in the Committee of Officers (COO) before approval by the Commission.” Why “henceforth”? Why was this not already being done by the COO and the Commission? Why were new initiatives, that deprived BLOs and EROs their vested rights, anchored in the avowed decentralized decision making,  to access ECINet system, were being taken without the approval/consultation with the Commission?

Seven : Meetings of ECs With CEC Without Agenda & Without Recording Minutes  
The Note says: “For all Commission’s meeting, agenda will be circulated in advance and minutes will be issued.” Why was this not being done in the past? If there is no record of meetings of the 2 ECs and CEC, how does one know whether what was disseminated downwards was what the CEC and 2 ECs had decided together  in their meetings? The Commission should place on record agendas and minutes of previous ten months meetings of the Commission.

Eight   
The Note says: “SIR has already been completed in 20 States/UTs including Bihar and West Bengal. Any person whose name has got left out during SIR or thereafter, and any person including young/ first-time voter, may apply to the concerned ERO, for inclusion of their names in the electoral rolls under the process of continuous updation.” But this should have been done prior to the Bihar and West Bengal assembly elections.

Nine: Torpedoing The Intervention of The Supreme Court In The SIR Exercise
The Express expose of 22nd Sept says: “Last week, the Commission told the court that there were 38 lakh appeals pending before the tribunals: 22.21 lakh filed by deleted voters seeking to be restored. The other 16.10 lakh were filed against voters the judicial officers had included, seeking their deletion. The Commission did not say who filed those. In August, he (Mr Sandhu) wrote to Commission officials asking, “Who was authorized to file these appeals on behalf of ECI, and by whom?” and “Who actually filed these appeals on behalf of ECI? He recorded that neither he nor Joshi nor the current Chief Electoral Officer of West Bengal had been informed about the basis or process of filing the appeals”.
The silence on this issue in the Commission’s Press Note of 26th Sept is hugely incriminating for the CEC/Commission because of  the  clear allegation, not insinuation, that the Commission was responsible for filing ~16 lakh appeal, without the approval of the 2 ECs, which made it unlawful under Chief Election Commissioner and Other Election Commissioners (Appointment, Conditions of Service and Term of Office) Act, 2023.  It also torpedoed the intervention of the Supreme Court which had got “judicial officers to decide on the plight of 60 lakh voters flagged for logical discrepancies.”

The Strength of  The Democratic Temper Of We the People Of India Should Not Be Tested
If  ~13 crore people who voted in the previous assembly elections cannot vote and their deleted names are not transferred anywhere else, if the number of eligible voters is decreasing in the Assembly Elections of 2025 and 2026 instead of naturally increasing (In Bihar assembly elections of 2025 the voter roll fell by 5.95%. In West Bengal elections of 2026 it fell by 12%), if decentralization of voter addition and deletion is superseded  by centralization under CEC, if a sitting Judge of Supreme Court can say in respect of lacs of voters whose names were deleted from voter list while their appeal was pending before the Tribunal that in “this election, yes, perhaps they can't vote...”, if cast iron immunity against criminal misconduct is given to CEC under Section 16 of the 2023 Act for life in addition to the protection given to CEC under Article 324(5) of the Constitution, if indeed the right to vote is arbitrarily and selectively taken away through a hubris infected  SIR, then we are heading towards a sham democracy.

There is additional danger of CEC brazening it out, and recommending removal of the two ECs which the President may accept upon the advice of the Council of Ministers. Let’s not forget the arrogance of the CEC which is built on the practical impossibility of removing him through the Constitutional process. Compared to the CEC, the ECs have virtually no protection from removal under Section 11(2) of the 2023 Act, should the CEC decide to initiate their removal.  

Not only is faith in the Election Commission being eroded, the Apex Court has been dragging its feet in the aftermath of its judgement in Anoop Baranwal’s case, which held that appointments to the EC cannot remain under “exclusive executive control”. Yet executive blessings, if not control, is writ large in the manner in which CEC has functioned. There is no urgency to decide the issue of current mechanism of selection of ECs and CEC in which the Executive has complete control,  which challenges the core of Baranwal's judgement. The Apex Court has also tied its own hands even in the face of clear  illegalities committed by the Commission/CEC as revealed in the Express expose. Practically speaking, if the CEC cannot be removed, at least the illegal orders of the CEC should have been set aside.

If these two constitutional pillars of democracy- Election Commission and Supreme Court – do not uphold the foundation of democracy, which starts with free and fair elections, then the gates to the Nepal and Bangladesh model could open. We have seen the success of the 2020-2021  farmer’s protest, and of the 2026 student’s protest. It would be a great mistake for stakeholders of  democracy to pooh pooh the possibility of the Nepal/ Bangladesh model, and continue to challenge the democratic temper of  “We, The People of India”.
